      "text": "To find the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the GCD of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\):**\n\n   The prime factorizations of the numbers are:\n   \\[\n   18 = 2 \\times 3^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n   \\]\n\n   The GCD is found by taking the lowest power of each prime factor present in both factorizations:\n   \\[\n   \\text{GCD} = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n   \\]\n\n2. **Find the LCM of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\):**\n\n   The LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime factor present in either number:\n   \\[\n   \\text{LCM} = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the product of the GCD and the LCM:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Product} = \\text{GCD} \\times \\text{LCM} = 6 \\times 126\n   \\]\n\n   Now, perform the multiplication:\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 126 = 756\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is \\(\\boxed{756}\\).",
