      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\), we start by determining the prime factorizations of these numbers.\n\nFirst, we factorize \\(18\\):\n\\[\n18 = 2 \\times 3^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we factorize \\(42\\):\n\\[\n42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n\\]\n\nThe greatest common divisor (GCD) is the product of the lowest powers of all prime factors that are common to both numbers. The common prime factors of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) are \\(2\\) and \\(3\\). The lowest power of \\(2\\) in both factorizations is \\(2^1\\), and the lowest power of \\(3\\) in both factorizations is \\(3^1\\). Therefore, the GCD is:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(18, 42) = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n\\]\n\nNow, we find the least common multiple (LCM). The LCM is the product of the highest powers of all prime factors that appear in the factorizations of the numbers. The prime factorizations are \\(18 = 2 \\times 3^2\\) and \\(42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\\). The highest power of \\(2\\) is \\(2^1\\), the highest power of \\(3\\) is \\(3^2\\), and the highest power of \\(7\\) is \\(7^1\\). Therefore, the LCM is:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\n\\]\n\nFinally, we calculate the product of the GCD and the LCM:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(18, 42) \\times \\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 6 \\times 126 = 756\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is \\(\\boxed{756}\\).",
