      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\), we need to determine these values first.\n\n### Step 1: Find the Greatest Common Divisor (GCD)\nThe GCD of two numbers is the largest number that divides both of them without leaving a remainder. We start by finding the prime factorizations of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\):\n\n\\[\n18 = 2 \\times 3^2\n\\]\n\\[\n42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n\\]\n\nThe prime factors of \\(18\\) are \\(2\\) and \\(3\\), and the prime factors of \\(42\\) are \\(2\\), \\(3\\), and \\(7\\). The common prime factors are \\(2\\) and \\(3\\). To find the GCD, we take the lowest power of each common prime factor:\n\n\\[\n\\text{GCD} = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Find the Least Common Multiple (LCM)\nThe LCM of two numbers is the smallest number that is a multiple of both. We use the prime factorizations again:\n\n\\[\n18 = 2^1 \\times 3^2\n\\]\n\\[\n42 = 2^1 \\times 3^1 \\times 7^1\n\\]\n\nThe LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime factor present in the factorizations:\n\n\\[\n\\text{LCM} = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the Product of the GCD and the LCM\nNow that we have the GCD and the LCM, we can find their product:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Product} = \\text{GCD} \\times \\text{LCM} = 6 \\times 126\n\\]\n\nTo calculate \\(6 \\times 126\\):\n\n\\[\n6 \\times 126 = 756\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is \\(\\boxed{756}\\).",
